Description: Save the session a few seconds after it changes, not only when the last window closes.
 Ptyxis saves its session, the windows with their tabs and the directory of each tab, only when
 the last window is closed or a window loses its last tab. When it ends any other way, as in a
 crash, a reset of the machine or at the end of the desktop session, where ptyxis-agent ends
 first and Ptyxis exits, the next start restores the session saved the last time all windows
 were closed, which can be days old.
 .
 The session is now saved 3 seconds after a change, with further changes until then included
 in the same save: a tab added, closed, moved, pinned or selected, its directory or title
 changed, a window closed, maximized or zoomed. Writes of the session file run one at a time,
 and a state equal to the one last written is not written again. Nothing is saved this way by a
 standalone instance, while a saved session is still to be restored, as when Ptyxis was started
 for a single command, nor once the last window is closed, which saves the session as before.
 .
 A window created for a single command is left out of the session, as it would be restored as
 a shell without its command.
Author: Mitar
Bug: https://gitlab.gnome.org/chergert/ptyxis/-/work_items/603
Forwarded: no
Last-Update: 2026-11-04
